<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Harold Innis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1894–1952</span> · Also known as Harold Adams Innis</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Harold Innis was a Canadian economic historian at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>. In works such as The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">Fur Trade</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> he developed the staples thesis, which explained Canada's development through the export of raw materials such as fur, fish, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a> and wheat. His later studies of communication, Empire and Communications and The Bias of Communication, influenced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marshall-mcluhan">Marshall McLuhan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/harold-innis.jpg" alt="Harold Innis" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: H. James that is a pseudonym for William James Jr. according to City of Toroto Archive · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Harold_Innis_public-domain_library_archives-canada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
